  "notes": "Career-long dance-pop vocalist turning nu-disco from 2000 onward; the 1987-1989 Stock Aitken Waterman period fails Gate 2 and is excluded, which is why the window opens in 1990. The harmony follows the documented preference for \"comfortable composition\" — functional, two- to four-chord, \"never obstructs the hook\" — so the families are major disco cadences with a minor alternative for the electro records, and the 7ths the entry names put `seventh` level with `triad`. The kit is the documented one: \"four-square kick with claps on 2&4, disco offbeat hats opening to 16ths, cowbell and tambourine as period markers\", which is the cowbell/tambourine perc lane and a weighted 8th/16th hat base rather than the parent's. The bass is authored hardest because the entry calls it \"the second hook\" — syncopated, independent of the kick at 0.7 roots. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the melody register and density, the perc densities and the open-hat share: \"short-celled, repeated, and pitched low enough to be spoken-sung\" is a direction, and 62-84 at 4-8 notes a bar is this author's reading. ⚠ The \"angelic light-soprano writing in a narrow, breathy band\" claim carries a [LOW-CONF] mark in the research and is only partly encoded, in the narrow interval spread rather than in a high register.",
